From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:50:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Govern the published performance state by
 utilization

The firmware runs at the state the three Globals selectors publish,
busy or idle, and the host published the thermal ceiling: on the J713
the GPU sat at S9 (1470 MHz, 1000 mV) with nothing to draw. Its rail
drew 1.06 W idle and 1.25 W for a 60 fps desktop, against 68-90 mW
for the same desktop at S1-S3.

Request a state from the GPU's busy share of each 20 ms window, between
S3 and the thermal and user ceiling, which still bound it: the top state
when a window was at least 70% busy, one step up at 40%, and down on a
utilization averaged over about four windows, one step under 25% and to
S3 under 10%. Busy time runs from publication to drain. An idle engine
polls once more per window until it is back at S3. S3 draws no more than
S1 on a desktop load and runs at 2.4 times its clock; S4 and up cost
several times as much.

The accounting is policy::Governor, which the host policy test runs
directly: publication to drain, busy spans carried across windows, flights
dropped by recovery without a drain, the idle re-arm back to S3, and
thermal and user ceilings, including those below S3.

Paired on the J713, on battery with the backlight at half (idle 5 x 60 s,
three 60 Hz scenes 3 x 30 s each, the glmark2 suite 3 times): idle draw
7.32 -> 6.06 W, the GPU rail 1.09 W -> 27 mW. The 60 Hz build scene stays
at S3 with no missed frame, and the glmark2 score is 574 -> 573.

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
[eryk: don't re-arm the idle poll on a failed engine: its poll returns
 before service_power(), so the demand never decays, and an engine that
 failed while idle above S3 re-armed its poll every 20 ms until reboot
 once anything kicked it; the host test's call-site check follows]
Signed-off-by: Eryk Wieliczko <eryk@wieliczko.ninja>
